Lay the poudriere ports tree named
.Ar overlay
over the main one, as
.Nm poudriere Cm bulk Fl O
does: its location is read from
.Pa /usr/local/etc/poudriere.d/ports/ Ns Ar overlay Ns Pa /mnt .
Repeatable; the first given shadows the next and the main tree.
See
.Sx OVERLAYS .
Needs
.Pa poudriere.d ;
mutually exclusive with
.Fl l
and
.Fl s .
.It Fl -overlay-dir Ar dir
Lay
.Ar dir
over the ports tree as an overlay, verbatim: the counterpart of
.Fl O
as
.Fl P
is of
.Fl p ,
for poudriere and local builds.
Repeatable, in order with
.Fl O .
Mutually exclusive with
.Fl s .
.It Fl l , Fl -local
Configure for a local build on this host, as
.Ql make config
would, ignoring
.Pa /usr/local/etc/poudriere.d
even when it exists.
See
.Sx LOCAL BUILDS .
Mutually exclusive with
.Fl z ,
.Fl j ,
.Fl p
and
.Fl s .
.It Fl s Oo Ar profile Oc , Fl -synth Oo Ar profile Oc
Configure for
.Xr synth 1
instead of
.Xr poudriere 8 ,
using the named profile; without one, synth.ini's
.Va profile_selected
is honoured, falling back to
.Ql LiveSystem .
See
.Sx SYNTH .
Mutually exclusive with
.Fl z ,
.Fl j ,
.Fl p
and
.Fl l .
Because the profile name is optional, a name that could be read as something
else
.Pq a subcommand, an option, or anything containing a slash
must be attached, as
.Ql --synth=name .
.It Fl o Ar dir , Fl -options-dir Ar dir
Use
.Ar dir
as the options directory verbatim, bypassing the poudriere, synth or local
resolution entirely.
A relative
.Ar dir ,
here as for
.Fl P ,
is taken from the current directory.
.It Fl f Ar pkglist , Fl -file Ar pkglist
Read port origins from
.Ar pkglist ,
one
.Ar origin Ns Op @ Ns Ar flavor
per line,
.Ql #
starting a comment
.Pq the Xr poudriere 8 pkglist format .
Repeatable; duplicates across files and command line arguments are dropped.
.It Fl J Ar jobs , Fl -jobs Ar jobs
Number of parallel
.Nm make
queries.
Defaults to
.Ic min Ns Pq 16 , Va ncpu .
.It Fl -no-cache
Neither read nor write the persistent query cache.
.It Fl -clear-cache
Delete the persistent query cache
.Pq every generation file, drafts left alone
before running; given alone, without a subcommand or a package list, clear
it and exit.
Needed after editing a file that a
.Pa make.conf
includes; see
.Sx CAVEATS .
.It Fl -color Ar when
When to colorize output:
.Cm auto
.Pq the default ,
.Cm always
or
.Cm never .
Under
.Cm auto
color is emitted only when standard output is a terminal and
.Ev NO_COLOR
is unset;
.Cm always
is an explicit request and overrides
.Ev NO_COLOR .
Colorized are the
.Cm scan
status marker column on standard output and the informational standard-error
output: banner labels, notes, warnings, errors and progress lines, with
tty-ness judged per stream.
The interactive TUI is unaffected.
.It Fl n , Fl -dry-run
Report what
.Cm sync
and
.Cm clean
would change without touching anything.
The TUI ignores this flag: its apply dialog previews every change instead.
.It Fl v , Fl -verbose
More detail per subcommand:
.Cm scan
prints each port's full
.Ql +ON
and
.Ql -OFF
option state plus query warnings,
.Cm sync
prints the final state of every written file, and
.Cm clean
explains why each kept entry is kept, for example which options deviate from
defaults plus
.Pa make.conf .
.It Fl m , Fl -minimal
Keep the options directory minimal on
.Cm sync
and TUI apply: a port whose final configuration equals what defaults plus
.Pa make.conf
already produce gets no options file.
An existing identical file is removed instead of rewritten, so only
deviations are persisted.
Bare defaults also count as decided:
.Cm scan
and the TUI then only flag deviations and conflicts, a port the defaults
and
.Pa make.conf
settle showing as done
.Po
.Ql ok (no file: defaults + make.conf)
in
.Cm scan ,
.Sq \(ap
in the TUI, as under its
.Ic m
view
.Pc
whether it has a file, a stale one, or none.
.It Fl q , Fl -quiet
Drop the startup banner and the per-port and per-file listings on standard
output.
The summary line and all warnings and errors on standard error remain.
Ignored by
.Cm scan Fl -json ;
mutually exclusive with
.Fl v .
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Print usage and exit.
.It Fl V , Fl -version
Print the version and exit.
.El
.Ss tui
.Nm Cm tui Oo Fl -drive Oc Oo Fl f Ar pkglist Oc Op Ar origin ...
.Pp
The default subcommand: running
.Nm
with port origins and no subcommand starts the interface.
It requires a real terminal and refuses to start when standard input or output
is not a tty, unless
.Fl -drive
is given
.Pq see Sx DRIVE PROTOCOL .
.Pp
The left pane lists every port in the dependency closure that has options
.Pq optionless ports remain in the graph but are never shown ,
problems first, each with two marker columns.
The first is the port's state:
.Bl -tag -width 6n -compact
.It \[u2717]
conflict: the staged options violate
.Va PREVENTS
or a group rule.
.It \&?
unconfigured: no saved options file.
.It \&!
stale: the option list changed since the file was written.
.It \[u2260]
contradicts the
.Pa make.conf
.Va OPTIONS_SET
or
.Va OPTIONS_UNSET
policy; shown only in the
.Ic w
view.
.It \[u2248]
needs no attention because
.Pa make.conf
already decides it; shown only in the
.Ic m
view.
.It \[u2713]
ok.
.El
.Pp
The second is
.Ql *
when the port has staged changes not yet applied, blank otherwise; an edit
hides no state, so an edited unconfigured port reads
.Ql ?* ,
and an edited ok port
.Ql \[u2713]* .
The order and
.Ic n ,
.Ic p
and
.Ic t
go by the state alone: edits are not a problem.
In problems-first order, when both kinds are listed, a row
.Ql \[u2500]\[u2500] N ok \[u2500]\[u2500]
parts the problem ports from the N ok ones
.Pq \[u2248] and \[u2713] ;
it is not a port, and no key selects it.
.Pp
Three further markers may follow the port's name:
.Sq \[u2298]
for a port blacklisted for this jail, tree and set, which therefore never needs
attention,
.Sq \[u26A0]
for a port that is
.Va BROKEN
or
.Va IGNORE Ns d
with its current options, and
.Sq \[u221E]
for a port caught in a dependency loop
.Pq see Sx Dependency loops .
A row too long for the pane loses its category first, keeping the port's
name, its
.Ql @flavor
and these markers whole, as in
.Ql multim\[u2026]/ffmpeg@default \[u26A0] ,
then
.Ql \[u2026]/ffmpeg@default \[u26A0] ;
still too long, the end of the name and flavor goes to a
.Ql \[u2026] ,
never a marker.
The list's title counts the ports listed and, when there are any, the
optionless ones it never shows, as in
.Ql Ports (21 \[u00B7] 20 optionless) ,
then the views narrowing it
.Pq Ql \[em] needs attention , Ql \[em] in a dependency loop .
When it does not fit, the optionless count goes first, the views being kept
before it, then the views are named in short
.Pq Ql \[em] attention , Ql \[em] in a loop ,
then the rest is cut with
.Ql \[u2026]
at a word.
A filter shows on the list's bottom border, at its right, as
.Ql filter: TEXT ;
one too long for the border loses its start to a
.Ql \[u2026] ,
so the end being typed stays in view.
The focused pane has a double border, the other a single one; the option
pane's inner rules join whichever it has.
.Pp
The bottom line is the status bar: the count of ports in each state, the
edited ones apart as
.Ql * ,
then the key legend, starting with
.Ql ?:help a:apply q:quit
and going on with the keys of the focused pane, cut at a whole key with
.Ql \[u2026]
where the width ends.
A message takes its place until the next key.
One set by background work
.Pq a re-query's result, a port turned Va BROKEN , a loop made or broken
does not replace a message set since the last key unless it is an error, and
only an error replaces an error.
At start the first message sums up what needs doing, as in
.Ql 3 conflicting \[u00B7] 4 never configured \[u00B7] 2 stale \[em] a: write defaults for all \[u00B7] n: next problem \[u00B7] ?: help ,
the
.Ql a:
part only when some ports were never configured, or
.Ql all 21 ports are configured \[em] ?: help
when nothing does.
.Pp
The right pane shows the port's options in framework order under their group
headers, whose kind is enforced on every toggle:
.Dv SINGLE
requires exactly one enabled member,
.Dv RADIO
at most one and
.Dv MULTI
at least one.
.Pp
Each option row starts with
.Ql N
when the option was added to the port since its options file was written, so
every such option can be found down the pane's left edge, and carries the
staged value, the port default
.Pq Ql on No or Ql off ,
and then a second
.Ql on Ns / Ns Ql off :
what
.Pa make.conf
says of the option, whatever decides its value now, the options file
included \(em the value of the last layer naming it, in the framework's
order: the global knob, then the per-port one, then the
.Va *_FORCE
knobs, the later winning \(em in the colour of that layer: green for a global
.Va OPTIONS_SET
or
.Va OPTIONS_UNSET ,
bright green for the per-port
.Va ${OPTIONS_NAME}_SET
or
.Va _UNSET ,
and red bold for a
.Va *_FORCE
knob that the options file cannot override, which also locks the toggle.
That column is empty only when no
.Pa make.conf
layer names the option.
Options auto-enabled through an
.Va IMPLIES
chain are locked and marked
.Ql implied by X ,
and options carrying
.Va <OPT>_BROKEN
or
.Va <OPT>_IGNORE
get a warning badge.
A yellow option name deviates from the port default; a magenta one contradicts
.Pa make.conf
policy \(em the colour carries that on its own, the row has no badge for it.
Options that no longer exist are struck through and dropped on apply; options
excluded by the current flavor but managed through the default flavor are
listed in their own section.
.Pp
Above the rows, a line each says what is wrong with the port or whose view it
is:
.Ql BROKEN: ,
.Ql IGNORE:
and the option changes likely to blame,
.Ql DEPRECATED: ,
the flavor owning its options file, a
.Ql CONFLICT:
per rule the staged options break, and
.Ql STALE:
when the port's option list changed since its file was written, naming the
options added and those gone
.Pq Ql STALE: new A B \[u00B7] obsolete X ,
each part only when it has options.
For a port the list counts as done
.Po
.Sq \[u2248]
under
.Fl m
or the
.Ic m
view, or
.Sq \[u2298]
.Pc
the
.Ql STALE:
line is dimmed and says what settles it, as in
.Ql STALE: obsolete X \(em settled by the defaults (-m) .
A
.Ql CONFLICT:
names the hand
.Pa make.conf
had in it, after a
.Ql \(em :
the members on because a
.Pa make.conf
layer sets them where the options file does not, and that layer
.Pq Ql A, B on by make.conf OPTIONS_SET ,
or
.Va ${OPTIONS_NAME}_SET
for the per-port knob; a member a
.Va *_FORCE
knob pins on
.Pq Ql A forced on by make.conf; fix it there ;
for a group with no member on, the members to pick from and those
.Pa make.conf
turns off
.Pq Ql pick one of A B C (A off by make.conf) .
A
.Dv PREVENTS
conflict keeps its own text, and gains the
.Pa make.conf
part when
.Pa make.conf
sets one of the two.
A group header or one of those lines too wide for the pane ends in
.Ql \[u2026]
after its last whole word rather than being cut mid-word at the border; a
.Ql CONFLICT:
or
.Ql STALE:
line first wraps to a second line, under its text, and is cut only past that.
An option row too wide drops only its trailing description: the checkbox,
the name, both values and the badges always stay, and a row still too wide
has the last of them cut in its own text at the border, as in
.Ql implied by AUTH\[u2026] ,
never with a
.Ql \[u2026]
after a whole column.
.Pp
The pane's title is the port's versioned package name, cut at a word with
.Ql \[u2026]
when it does not fit; the origin is the list's row.
A flavored port's title lists its flavors after
.Ql flavors: ,
marking with
.Ql *
the flavor whose view owns the shared options file and with
.Ql \[u2039]
the flavor shown in the pane.
A flavor the closure holds, one
.Ic f
reaches, is underlined; one the port declares but the closure does not hold
is plain.
.Pp
The pane's bottom border names, on its left, the overlay a port is taken
from, as
.Ql overlay: NAME ;
on its right, the port's staged changes against its saved file, as
.Ql +OPT -OPT ,
additions first, and, while the pane has the focus and the cursor is on an
option, the cursor's place among the port's options, as
.Ql 3/12 ,
the two parted by
.Ql \[u00B7] .
Where they do not fit, the overlay's name is cut first, then the changes lose
their start to a
.Ql \[u2026] ;
the place always shows.
A port with none of them has a plain border.
.Pp
A panel at the bottom of the pane, one line high on a pane 56 columns wide or
more and two on a narrower one, follows the cursor: titled with the
name of the option under it, it gives that option's description as the ports
framework states it, wrapped to the pane instead of cut short as the rows
are.
Because of that panel a row keeps its trailing description only while a useful
amount of it fits \(em under about sixteen columns of room the row drops it
rather than show a word fragment, and one too long ends in
.Ql \[u2026]
at a word, or goes when not even its first two words fit.
An option the framework describes nowhere reads
.Ql (no description) .
The panel marks with an ellipsis anything it cannot fit, and keeps its place
and height whatever the option, so the rows never move; while no option is
highlighted it stays, untitled, and reads
.Ql Enter: edit the options .
.Pp
Toggling an option re-queries the port in the background against a staging
.Va PORT_DBDIR ,
so dependencies that the option adds or removes appear or disappear within a
second or two.
Nothing touches the real options directory until
.Ic a
is pressed, which previews every file as a diff and then writes every file,
or with
.Ic s
every file but the conflicting ports', atomically
.Pq temporary file, Xr fsync 2 , Xr rename 2 .
A port whose staged options violate their constraints makes its options file
conflicting, and so does any flavor sharing it; when the pass would write
one, the preview reads
.Ql apply with N conflicting? ,
lists those ports first, and
.Ic y
writes nothing.
.Ic Y
writes every file, conflicting ones included;
.Ic s
leaves the conflicting files as they are on disk, neither written nor removed,
and their ports' edits staged, still marked
.Ql \[u2717] ,
and kept in the draft a quit offers to save.
If any file cannot be written, none is and nothing is removed: the result
names the failure and the edits stay staged.
Each options file is read once, at start, and judged as it was then: a file
edited outside
.Nm
while the interface is open is left alone by apply unless its port is edited
in the interface too.
.Pp
Key bindings:
.Bl -tag -width "Enter, Tab" -compact
.It Ic j , k , \(ua , \(da
Move the selection;
.Ic g
and
.Ic G
jump to the first and last entry,
.Ic PgUp
and
.Ic PgDn ,
or
.Ic Ctrl-B
and
.Ic Ctrl-F ,
page by the rows shown, less one.
A Ctrl-letter is never read as its plain letter.
.It Ic Enter , Tab , \(->
Edit the selected port, the cursor on its first option needing attention:
for a conflicting port, the first member, in row order, of the first rule it
breaks; else, for a stale one, its first new option
.Pq Ql N ;
else its first option, never a group header.
Reaching another port from the option pane, with
.Ic n ,
.Ic p
or a jump from
.Ic r ,
lands the same way;
.Ic f
keeps the option under the cursor when the other flavor has it.
.Ic Esc ,
.Ic Tab
or
.Ic \(<-
returns to the list.
.It Ic Space
Toggle the selected option, enforcing group rules.
.Ic Enter
does nothing in the option pane.
On the port list
.Ic Space
toggles nothing and says
.Ql Enter: edit the options .
.It Ic d
Reset the port to its defaults, from either pane.
.It Ic U
Revert the port to its saved state, from either pane.
.It Ic u
Undo the last option change, whichever port it was on and whatever made it
.Pq a toggle, Ic d , Ic U , a bulk decision or a restored draft .
Up to 100 changes back; the affected port is re-queried like any other edit.
.It Ic B
Bulk-set one option across the listed ports, those of them that have it.
The prompt accepts
.Ar OPTION Ns Cm = Ns Cm on Ns | Ns Cm off ,
or the shorthands
.Ar OPTION Ns Cm +
and
.Ar OPTION Ns Cm - ,
prefilled with
.Ar OPTION Ns Cm =
when the cursor is on an option.
Its title says which ports it works on,
.Ql bulk over the N listed ports ,
followed by
.Ql (filter: TEXT) ,
.Ql (attention only)
or
.Ql (in a loop)
for what narrows them; its bottom border counts, as you type, the listed
ports carrying the option named so far
.Pq Ql M carry OPTION ,
or says in red
.Ql no listed port has OPTION .
.Ic Ctrl-U
clears the line,
.Ic Enter
applies and
.Ic Esc
