.Ic Esc
or any other key stays in the interface.
The next session on the same options directory restores the draft's staged
edits, dropping options that meanwhile vanished from a port and re-querying the
restored ports so their dependencies reappear, and its first message leads
with the draft, as in
.Ql restored draft (2 ports, 5m ago, U per port reverts) ,
the last part dropped first on a narrow screen.
The draft is cleared as soon as the changes are applied or discarded, or the
session quits with nothing staged;
.Ic U
still reverts an individual port.
.Ss PORT NOTES
A port may ship a
.Pa pkg-help
file next to its
.Pa Makefile
.Pq Va PKGHELP , No that is Pa ${PKGDIR}/pkg-help :
free-form notes about its options, which
.Xr make 1 Cm config
offers behind the Help button of
.Xr portconfig 1 .
Roughly thirty ports in a tree have one
.Pq Pa www/apache24 , Pa security/gnupg , Pa lang/gcc* , Pa mail/postfix No among them .
.Pp
Ports that have one advertise it with a
.Ql [h] notes
marker at the right of the option pane's title, and
.Ic h
opens it from either pane.
Whether the file exists is settled when the port is queried \(em a cache
generation is one ports-tree commit, so it cannot appear or vanish behind a
cached entry \(em but its contents are read from disk every time the popup is
opened, so edits show up without restarting
.Nm .
.Pp
Note that per option the framework offers nothing but the description
.Pq Va <OPT>_DESC ,
which the option pane already shows in full;
.Ic F1
in
.Xr portconfig 1
opens that program's own help about the ports collection, not the port's.
.Ss DRIVE PROTOCOL
.Nm Cm tui Fl -drive Oo Fl f Ar pkglist Oc Op Ar origin ...
.Pp
With
.Fl -drive
the interface runs headless, for debugging and end-to-end testing: the closure
is scanned as usual, but instead of taking over a terminal it renders into an
in-memory screen
.Pq 100 by 35 cells by default
and reads one command per line from standard input.
The keymap, the application state and the drawing code are the interactive
ones, so what a dump shows is what a terminal would show.
.Pp
Blank lines and lines starting with
.Ql #
are ignored.
Every other line is acknowledged on standard output with a line starting with
.Ql ok
or
.Ql err ,
except
.Cm dump
and
.Cm state ,
which print their payload instead.
Output is flushed after each command, so the driver can be held open on a pipe.
A malformed command is reported and never ends the session.
Pending background re-queries are merged before each command, exactly as the
interactive event loop does on every tick, and a frame is drawn before each
key, as that loop draws one, so the page keys move by what the screen shows.
.Bl -tag -width 16n
.It Cm key Ar spec
Press one key.
.Ar spec
is an optional
.Ql ctrl-
and/or
.Ql alt-
prefix followed by either a single character or one of
.Cm enter esc tab backtab space backspace up down left right pgup pgdn home end
.Cm f1 Ns \(en Ns Cm f12 question slash .
Case matters for characters:
.Ql B
is the bulk key,
.Ql b
is not.
.It Cm keys Ar text
Press each character of
.Ar text
in turn, spaces included, for typing into the filter or bulk prompts.
.It Cm dump
Draw a frame and print the screen as
.Ql screen Ar W Ns x Ns Ar H ,
then one line per row with trailing blanks stripped, then
.Ql end .
.It Cm state
Print a one-line JSON object with the fields
.Va focus , selected , visible , listable , refreshing , pending , message ,
.Va overlay , filter , dirty
and
.Va loops
.Pq how many dependency loops the closure has .
.Va overlay
names the popup on screen, if any.
.It Cm settle Op Ar timeout_ms
Wait until no background re-query is outstanding
.Pq default 5000 ms ,
answering
.Ql ok settled
or
.Ql err settle timeout .
This is also what lets the 300 ms debounce on option edits expire.
.It Cm resize Ar W Ns x Ns Ar H
Recreate the in-memory screen at that size, clamped to 20\(en500 cells.
.It Cm quit
End the session; so does end of file on standard input, or any key the
interface itself treats as quitting.
.El
.Pp
Nothing is written to the options directory unless the script drives the apply
flow, so pointing
.Fl o
at a scratch directory keeps a session harmless.
.Ss scan
.Nm Cm scan Oo Fl -json Oc Oo Fl f Ar pkglist Oc Op Ar origin ...
.Pp
Non-interactive status report over the closure, intended as a build gate.
Each port is classified as
.Cm ok ,
.Cm unconfigured
.Pq no saved options file ,
.Cm stale
.Pq the option list changed since the file was written, with the added and removed options named ,
or
.Cm conflict
.Pq the recorded options violate Va PREVENTS No or a group constraint .
Every flagged port also lists the options that
.Pa make.conf
does
.Em not
decide, as
.Ql undecided: ... ,
or is annotated
.Ql [mc-covered \[u2248]]
when
.Pa make.conf
decides all of them.
Ports that are fully
.Cm mc-covered
never demand attention, and neither do blacklisted ports: poudriere will not
build them in this jail, tree and set, so their options cannot hold a build up.
Ports without any options are counted separately and not listed.
.Pp
A port caught in a dependency loop
.Pq see Sx Dependency loops
keeps its own status and gains a
.Ql [dependency loop]
annotation; the loop itself is named on standard error and counted in the
summary.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -json
Print one machine-readable JSON object on standard output instead of the table;
progress and the summary stay on standard error and
.Fl q
is ignored.
The object has the keys
.Va options_dir ,
.Va ports_tree ,
.Va overlays
.Pq the trees laid over it, in lookup order ,
.Va ports ,
.Va loops ,
.Va summary
and
.Va errors ,
the ports whose query failed, each with
.Va subject
and
.Va message ;
such a port is missing from
.Va ports ,
and so is whatever it depends on.
Each element of
.Va ports
has
.Va port
.Pq Ar origin Ns Oo @ Ns Ar flavor Oc ,
.Va pkgname ,
.Va status
.Pq Ql conflict , Ql unconfigured , Ql stale No or Ql ok ,
.Va undecided ,
.Va added
and
.Va removed
.Pq both for stale ports ,
.Va mc_covered ,
.Va blacklisted ,
.Va in_loop ,
.Va options_file
.Pq the options-dir entry it reads
and
.Va overlay
.Pq the overlay the port is taken from, null for the ports tree .
Non-ok ports come first, then alphabetical order.
Each element of
.Va loops
has
.Va ports
.Pq every port of the tangle ,
.Va cycle
.Pq a shortest closed walk through it, its first port listed once
and
.Va blacklisted ,
true when every member is blacklisted and the loop therefore holds nothing up.
.Va summary
holds the counts
.Va total ,
.Va unconfigured ,
.Va stale ,
.Va conflict ,
.Va ok ,
.Va optionless ,
.Va blacklisted ,
.Va attention
.Pq ports owing a decision ,
.Va loops ,
.Va loops_blocking
.Pq loops with a member that is not blacklisted ,
.Va errors
.Pq ports whose query failed
and
.Va pending ,
which is
.Va attention
plus
.Va loops_blocking
plus
.Va errors
and drives the exit status.
.It Fl -options
With
.Fl -json ,
give every port its full per-option detail as well: two further keys per port,
.Va violations
.Pq why the status is Cm conflict
and
.Va options ,
an array in the framework's option order.
Each element has
.Va name ,
.Va staged
.Pq the value that would be written ,
.Va default
.Pq the port's own default ,
.Va saved
.Pq the value the options file records, null when it has no entry for it ,
.Va new
.Pq added to the port since the file was written ,
.Va makeconf ,
.Va locked
with
.Va locked_by ,
.Va deviates_makeconf ,
.Va group ,
.Va implies ,
.Va prevents ,
.Va prevents_msg ,
.Va desc ,
.Va broken ,
.Va ignore ,
.Va adds_deps
and
.Va uses .
.Va makeconf
is
.Bro Va value , Va scope Brc
with the scope
.Ql global ,
.Ql port
or
.Ql force ,
and is absent when no layer mentions the option;
.Va group
is
.Bro Va name , Va kind , Va members Brc
with the kind
.Ql group , Ql multi , Ql single No or Ql radio ;
.Va adds_deps
lists the port origins the option declares as dependencies.
That is everything a decision needs without running
.Nm make
again \(em see
.Sx MACHINE INTERFACE .
.El
.Pp
With
.Fl q
the per-port rows and the banner are dropped, leaving only the summary and any
warnings on standard error, which makes
.Nm Fl q Cm scan
usable directly as a
.Xr cron 8
gate that speaks up only when something is pending.
See
.Sx EXIT STATUS .
.Ss sync
.Nm Cm sync Oo Fl n Oc Oo Fl f Ar pkglist Oc Op Ar origin ...
.Pp
Headless refresh of the options files over the closure: the fast equivalent of
.Nm poudriere Cm options Fl C .
For every port it keeps the saved file's choices for options that still exist,
adopts the effective value
.Pq defaults plus Pa make.conf
for options the file does not know about, and drops options that no longer
exist.
The file is read the way
.Pa bsd.options.mk
reads it: an option on both an
.Va OPTIONS_FILE_SET
and an
.Va OPTIONS_FILE_UNSET
line is off, and an option on neither, named only in
.Va _FILE_COMPLETE_OPTIONS_LIST ,
keeps its effective value.
It then reports every file it changed.
A port whose options file already records the same complete option list and the
same enabled set is left untouched; a changed package name alone does not force
a rewrite, matching
.Nm make Cm config-conditional .
A file that names an option with neither line, which
.Nm make Cm config
never writes, is warned about by every command that reads it and completed
by
.Cm sync :
.Xr make 1
reads such an option at its defaults, follows them if they change later, and
.Nm make Cm config-conditional
asks about it again.
.Pp
Options files belonging to ports that lost
.Em all
of their options are removed as dead configuration, but only when no port in
the closure sharing that options file still has options, so a flavor with an
empty option list never deletes the default flavor's file.
Removal deletes the
.Pa options
file and the containing directory, but keeps a user-managed
.Pa options.local
if one is present.
.Pp
All flavors of one origin share a single options file.
It is written from the default flavor's point of view, exactly as
.Nm make config
on the plain origin would; a non-default flavor that disagrees on an option
both flavors carry is reported as a warning rather than silently overwritten.
Disagreements confined to options one flavor does not even have, such as those
removed by
.Va OPTIONS_EXCLUDE ,
are structural and not reported.
.Pp
With
.Fl n
nothing is written and the count of files that would be written and removed is
reported instead.
A later interactive session can revisit anything
.Cm sync
decided.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -json
Report on standard output as one JSON object instead of the per-file listing:
the same shape
.Cm decide
answers with, described under
.Sx MACHINE INTERFACE .
The summary and any warnings stay on standard error and
.Fl q
is ignored.
.El
.Ss decide
.Nm Cm decide Oo Fl n Oc Oo Fl f Ar pkglist Oc Op Ar origin ...
.Pp
Set option values from a plan read on standard input, for scripts and agents
that decide without a terminal.
The plan is one JSON object of ports, each an object of option names to
booleans:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{ "www/nginx": { "LUA": true, "DOCS": false },
  "devel/git@lite": { "CONTRIB": false } }
.Ed
.Pp
Values go through the same rules the interface enforces \(em group kinds, the
.Va IMPLIES
closure, the
.Va PREVENTS
check, and the
.Va *_FORCE
and implied locks \(em so a plan can never record a state
.Cm tui
would refuse.
Options are applied in name order within each port, which fixes the outcome
when one implies another or they share a single-choice group.
.Pp
The port list is optional: given none, the plan's own ports are the closure
roots, so a plan is self-contained.
Given one
.Pq positional origins and/or Fl f Ar pkglist ,
the closure comes from it and a plan naming a port outside it is reported as
unknown rather than guessed at.
.Pp
Only the options files of the ports the plan names are written;
.Cm sync
refreshes the rest.
Writing a port's file necessarily adopts its current option list, exactly as
.Cm sync
would: an option the tree has added since the file was written cannot be left
unrecorded.
.Pp
Nothing is written unless the plan is honoured in full.
A value the port's rules refuse, a value that some other value in the plan
undoes, a port the closure does not hold, or a resulting configuration that
violates
.Va PREVENTS
or a group rule all leave the options dir untouched and are reported in the
.Va rejected ,
.Va unknown
and
.Va conflicts
lists, with the exit status at 1.
Asking for a value a port already has is not a change and not an error, so a
plan can be replayed.
.Pp
The report always goes to standard output as JSON
.Pq see Sx MACHINE INTERFACE ;
.Fl n
previews it without writing anything.
.Ss clean
.Nm Cm clean Oo Fl n Oc Oo Fl r Oc Oo Fl u Oc Oo Fl f Ar pkglist Oc Op Ar origin ...
.Pp
Garbage-collect the resolved options directory.
By default it walks every entry in the directory and removes those whose port
has vanished from the ports tree, resolving the tree's
.Pa MOVED
file first so that renames and removals are named and explained rather than
guessed.
Plain
.Cm clean
needs no package list, and giving origins or
.Fl f
without
.Fl u
is an error, since it would ignore them.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl r , Fl -redundant
Also remove files whose recorded configuration is exactly what defaults plus
the
.Pa make.conf
overlay produce anyway, so that removing them changes nothing.
.It Fl u , Fl -unused
Also remove entries for ports outside the dependency closure of the given
package list
.Pq positional origins and/or Fl f Ar pkglist ,
which is then required.
If any port in that closure fails to query, the closure is incomplete and
.Nm
refuses to prune anything rather than risk deleting live configuration.
.It Fl -json
Report on standard output as one JSON object instead of the per-entry listing:
.Va options_dir ,
.Va dry_run ,
.Va removals
.Pq each Bro Va options_name , Va reason Brc ,
.Va kept
.Pq only under Fl v ,
.Va warnings ,
.Va errors
and
.Va summary
.Pq Va entries , Va removed , Va failed .
The summary line stays on standard error and
.Fl q
is ignored.
.El
.Sh MACHINE INTERFACE
Every subcommand but
.Cm tui
is drivable without a terminal, and every one of them can answer in JSON, so a
script or an agent never has to read a screen or parse a table.
Two commands carry the work:
.Cm scan Fl -json Fl -options
says what the closure is and what each option means, and
.Cm decide
sets values from a plan.
One object per invocation goes to standard output and nothing else does;
progress, the banner, warnings and the summary all stay on standard error, so
stdout is safe to pipe into a parser without
.Fl q .
.Pp
The loop is:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# 1. what needs deciding, and what are the choices?
optique scan -z ws --json --options -f pkglist > state.json

# 2. decide (this is where the caller's judgement goes)
jq ... < state.json > plan.json

# 3. check the plan, then carry it out
optique decide -z ws -n < plan.json
optique decide -z ws    < plan.json
.Ed
.Pp
The contract is meant to be safe to drive blind:
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
Exit status is the answer to "is there anything left to do":
.Cm scan
exits 1 while a decision is pending,
.Cm decide
exits 1 when it did not honour the plan.
See
.Sx EXIT STATUS .
.It
.Cm decide
is all-or-nothing.
A plan that cannot be carried out in full changes nothing, so a caller is
never left having to work out which half of it landed.
.It
Everything refused is named.
A value the rules turn down, a value another value in the plan undoes, an
option the port does not have, a port outside the closure, a resulting
conflict: each appears in
.Va rejected ,
.Va unknown
or
.Va conflicts
with a reason, instead of being dropped.
.It
Plans are replayable.
Asking for the value a port already has is not a change, so the same plan can
be applied twice and the second run writes nothing.
.It
.Fl n
previews.
.Cm decide Fl n
and
.Cm sync Fl n
build the whole report, including the per-file diff, and touch nothing.
.El
.Pp
.Cm scan
answers the questions a decision needs: which options are undecided
.Pq Va undecided ,
what the value would be if nothing were decided
.Pq Va staged ,
whether
.Pa make.conf
has already settled it
.Pq Va makeconf , Va mc_covered ,
whether it can be changed at all
.Pq Va locked ,
what enabling it drags in
.Pq Va adds_deps
and what the framework says it is for
.Pq Va desc .
.Pp
.Cm sync
and
.Cm clean
take
.Fl -json
too, and report in the same shapes:
.Cm sync
answers exactly like
.Cm decide
.Pq with Va rejected , Va conflicts No and Va unknown No always empty , since it
asks for nothing ,
which makes the two interchangeable to a caller that only wants to know what
changed on disk.
.Pp
.Cm decide
and
.Cm sync
report
.Va options_dir ,
.Va dry_run ,
.Va applied
.Pq whether the options dir really changed ,
.Va writes ,
.Va removals ,
.Va rejected ,
.Va conflicts ,
.Va unknown ,
.Va warnings ,
.Va errors
and
.Va summary
.Pq Va written , Va removed , Va failed , Va rejected , Va conflicts .
Each element of
.Va writes
has
.Va port ,
.Va options_name ,
.Va file
.Pq its absolute path ,
.Va new_file ,
.Va diff
.Pq Ql +OPT No and Ql -OPT No for options the file already knew ,
.Va adopted
.Pq options the port gained since, each Bro Va name , Va on Brc ; empty for a new file, whose whole state is Va enabled ,
.Va dropped
.Pq options the port no longer has ,
.Va enabled
.Pq the complete set the file will record
and
.Va new_deps
.Pq origins the newly enabled options declare as dependencies .
.Pp
For driving the interface itself \(em to reproduce a rendering bug, or to test
it in CI \(em
.Cm tui Fl -drive
is a keystroke-level protocol over the real keymap and drawing code; see
.Sx DRIVE PROTOCOL .
It is a debugging tool, not the way to configure ports from a script:
.Cm decide
is.
.Sh POUDRIERE INTEGRATION
All poudriere state is read from
.Pa /usr/local/etc/poudriere.d .
Its existence is what selects poudriere: without it, or under
.Fl l ,
.Nm
configures a local build instead
.Pq see Sx LOCAL BUILDS ,
and
.Fl j ,
.Fl z
or
.Fl p
without it is an error.
.Fl o
bypasses the options directory resolution below, and
.Fl P
the ports tree lookup.
.Pp
Given
.Fl j Ar jail ,
.Fl p Ar tree
and
.Fl z Ar set ,
the options directory is resolved exactly as
.Xr poudriere 8
resolves it before null-mounting one over the jail's
.Pa /var/db/ports .
The candidates are tried in this order and the
.Em first existing
one wins:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
<jail>-<tree>-<set>-options
<jail>-<set>-options
<jail>-<tree>-options
<tree>-<set>-options
<set>-options
<tree>-options
<jail>-options
options
.Ed
.Pp
If none of them exists, applying creates the one
.Nm poudriere Cm options
would have used for the same flags,
.Pa [<jail>-][<tree>-][<set>-]options ,
the tree component only when
.Fl p
was given explicitly.
.Pp
.Pa make.conf
is layered in
.Xr poudriere 8 Ns 's
inclusion order, from least to most specific, with
.Em every
existing file merged, not just the first match:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
make.conf
<set>-make.conf
<tree>-make.conf
<jail>-make.conf
<tree>-<set>-make.conf
<jail>-<tree>-make.conf
<jail>-<set>-make.conf
<jail>-<tree>-<set>-make.conf
.Ed
.Pp
Port queries therefore see exactly what a poudriere build would see, including
.Va DEFAULT_VERSIONS ,
whose entries can change a port's default options, its option list and its
dependencies; a set that overrides for instance
.Ql DEFAULT_VERSIONS+=python=3.12
is honoured throughout the scan, the interface and every written file.
The same holds for the
.Va OPTIONS_SET ,
.Va OPTIONS_UNSET ,
per-port
.Va <PORT>_SET
and
.Va <PORT>_UNSET
and the
.Va *_FORCE
knobs, which is what the
.Ql mc ,
.Ql mc:port
and
.Ql FORCED
badges report.
.Pp
The layers are concatenated into one staged file, as poudriere concatenates
them into the jail's
.Pa make.conf ,
so an
.Ic .include
with a relative path does not find its file here any more than it would in
the jail; synth's profile
.Pa make.conf
is handled the same way.
The query cache is keyed on the text of the
.Pa make.conf
files, not of the files they include: after editing an included file, run
once with
.Fl -clear-cache .
.Pp
The layers are wrapped in the environment of a
.Xr poudriere 8
jail, not the host's: they come after the lines poudriere puts at the top
of every jail's
.Pa make.conf
.Pq Va PACKAGE_BUILDING , PACKAGE_BUILDING_FLAVORS , BATCH , USE_PACKAGE_DEPENDS
and are followed by
.Ql LOCALBASE=/nonexistent .
A jail has nothing installed when poudriere reads a port, while the host
running
.Nm
may; port Makefiles that probe installed files would otherwise answer for
the host, as
.Pa www/apache24
does when it marks LDAP IGNORE because the host's apr-util was built
without it.
When
.Va NO_PACKAGE_BUILDING
is set in
.Pa poudriere.conf
.Po
or in the
.Pa poudriere.d
files
.Xr poudriere 8
reads after it, for the jail, tree and set given
.Pc ,
.Va PACKAGE_BUILDING
and
.Va PACKAGE_BUILDING_FLAVORS
are left out, as poudriere leaves them out.
A local build
.Pq Sx LOCAL BUILDS
has no jail: queries see the host as it is, which is then the machine that
builds.
.Pp
The
.Pa blacklist
chain is read with the same jail, tree and set expansion and, again like
.Xr poudriere 8 ,
every existing file contributes entries:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
blacklist
<set>-blacklist
<tree>-blacklist
<jail>-blacklist
<tree>-<set>-blacklist
<jail>-<tree>-blacklist
<jail>-<set>-blacklist
<jail>-<tree>-<set>-blacklist
.Ed
.Pp
Each line holds one port origin,
.Ql #
starts a comment, and
.Ql *
globs are allowed, as in
.Ql www/nginx* .
Blacklisted ports are marked in
.Cm scan
and in the interface, never demand attention and never trip
.Cm scan Ns 's
exit status gate, whatever their status.
.Ss OVERLAYS
An overlay is a ports tree laid over the main one, holding only the ports it
adds or replaces, and possibly
.Pa Mk/Uses
files or a
.Pa MOVED ;
it is registered like any tree, with
.Nm poudriere Cm ports Fl c Fl p Ar name ,
and used by
.Nm poudriere Cm bulk Fl O Ar name
.Pq poudriere 3.4.0 or later .
.Nm poudriere Cm options
has no
.Fl O ,
so
.Nm
is the way to configure an overlay's ports:
.Dl optique scan -z server -O myoverlay -f list
.Pp
Each
.Fl O
is read from its
.Pa mnt
file, each
.Fl -overlay-dir
taken as given, in command-line order; neither needs a
.Pa Mk
directory.
They reach
.Xr make 1
as
.Ql OVERLAYS+=
lines where
.Xr poudriere 8
writes them in the jail's
.Pa make.conf ,
after its own lines and before the
.Pa poudriere.d
layers, with the host's paths.
The overlay list is then what make reports for that
.Pa make.conf ,
so an
.Va OVERLAYS
set by a layer counts too; the startup banner lists them in order.
A port is taken from the first overlay holding
.Pa <origin>/Makefile ,
else from the main tree, as the ports framework looks up dependencies:
a port only an overlay holds is found, as a root or a dependency, and
.Cm clean
counts a port as vanished only when no tree holds it.
.Cm scan
ends the row of a port an overlay answers for with
.Ql [overlay Ar name Ns ] ,
and the interface's option pane names it on its bottom border.
.Pa MOVED
is the main tree's then each overlay's, read as one file: a chain may run
across them, and an origin listed twice keeps its first entry, with a
warning.
The options directory, the
.Pa make.conf
layers and the blacklist do not depend on overlays.
The query cache is keyed on every tree, an overlay by the count and newest
modification time of its files down to
.Pa category/port/file ,
even when it is a git checkout of its own: an overlay port is edited in
place, before any commit.
.Pp
A local build takes
.Va OVERLAYS
from
.Pa /etc/make.conf
or the environment, as
.Xr make 1
does;
.Fl -overlay-dir
comes before those.
.Ss SYNTH
.Fl s Oo Ar profile Oc
reads the
.Xr synth 1
layout instead, so the same commands serve a synth host:
.Dl optique scan -s -f list
The profile defaults to synth.ini's
.Va profile_selected ,
then
.Ql LiveSystem .
The ports tree is taken from
.Va Directory_portsdir
and the options directory from
.Va Directory_options
in the
.Bq Ar profile
section of
.Pa /usr/local/etc/synth/synth.ini ,
falling back to that file's
.Bq Global Configuration
section and then to
.Pa /usr/ports
and
.Pa /var/db/ports .
.Pa make.conf
is the single file
.Pa /usr/local/etc/synth/ Ns Ar profile Ns Pa -make.conf ,
the one synth appends to each builder's
.Pa make.conf ,
or none at all when the profile has none: the host's
.Pa /etc/make.conf
is never read.
There is no layering, and synth has no blacklist.
.Fl o
and
.Fl P
still override the options directory and the ports tree;
.Fl p
names a poudriere tree and is refused with
.Fl s .
.Pp
synth reads each port in a chroot whose
.Pa /usr/local
is empty, so the queries do too: the profile's file comes after the lines
synth puts at the top of that chroot's
.Pa make.conf
.Po
.Va SYNTHPROFILE , PACKAGE_BUILDING , BATCH , USE_PACKAGE_DEPENDS_ONLY ,
but not
.Va PACKAGE_BUILDING_FLAVORS
.Pc
and is followed by
.Ql LOCALBASE=/nonexistent .
Port Makefiles that probe installed files would otherwise answer for the
host: with php82 installed,
.Pa Mk/Uses/php.mk
reads its
.Pa php.conf
and every php84 extension builds against
.Pa lang/php82 .
.Pp
When no ports are given at all, synth mode uses the installed packages as
the list, mirroring how synth builds what is installed: one root per
installed package, its origin from
.Ql pkg query -a %o
and its flavor from the package's
.Ql flavor
annotation, so several flavors of one origin installed side by side are
each a root.
This applies to the TUI,
.Cm scan ,
.Cm sync
and
.Cm clean Fl -unused .
.Fl -repo Ar name
is repeatable and restricts that implicit list to packages installed from
the named pkg repositories, e.g.
.Ql --repo Synth
to consider only synth-built packages; the names are listed by
.Ql pkg query -a \&%R .
It is an error wherever that list is not used: with ports given as
arguments or with
.Fl f ,
with
.Cm decide ,
and with
.Cm clean
without
.Fl -unused .
.Pp
Note that when
.Va Directory_options
is the live
.Pa /var/db/ports ,
minimal and redundancy cleanups also affect plain
.Ql make config
workflows.
.Ss LOCAL BUILDS
Without
.Pa /usr/local/etc/poudriere.d ,
or under
.Fl l
whether it exists or not,
.Nm
configures ports for a build on this host, the way
.Ql make config
does:
.Bl -bullet -offset indent
.It
the ports tree is
.Ev PORTSDIR
and the options directory
.Ev PORT_DBDIR ,
both as
.Xr make 1
sees them, from the environment or
.Pa /etc/make.conf ,
falling back to
.Pa /usr/ports
and
.Pa /var/db/ports ;
the startup banner notes a value that came from either variable, and
.Fl P
and
.Fl o
override them;
.It
the one
.Pa make.conf
layer is
.Pa /etc/make.conf ,
or the file
.Ev __MAKE_CONF
names in the environment, read in place so that its relative
.Ic .include Ns s
resolve; the cache still ignores the content of included files;
.It
queries see the host's installed packages under
.Pa /usr/local ,
since they are what the build will find
.Pq no Ql LOCALBASE=/nonexistent ,
so a package install or delete starts a new cache generation: the size and
modification time of
.Pa local.sqlite
in
.Ev PKG_DBDIR ,
else
.Pa /var/db/pkg ,
are part of the cache key;
.It
there is no blacklist.
.El
.Pp
.Fl l
is the way to configure a local build on a host that also runs poudriere:
.Dl optique scan -l -f list
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ev OVERLAYS
Overlays of a local build, as
.Xr make 1
reads them, after
.Fl -overlay-dir ;
see
.Sx OVERLAYS .
Made absolute and written into the build's
.Pa make.conf ,
never passed to a query through the environment; ignored by poudriere and
synth builds, whose builders do not see it.
.It Ev PORTSDIR , Ev PORT_DBDIR
The ports tree and options directory of a local build, from the
environment or
.Pa /etc/make.conf ;
see
.Sx LOCAL BUILDS .
Ignored by poudriere and synth builds, and never passed through to the
queries, which are always given the tree and options directory
.Nm
resolved.
.It Ev __MAKE_CONF
The
.Pa make.conf
of a local build, instead of
.Pa /etc/make.conf ;
see
.Sx LOCAL BUILDS .
Poudriere and synth builds always pass their own.
.It Ev PKG_DBDIR
Where a local build finds pkg's database, whose changes start a
new cache generation; see
.Sx LOCAL BUILDS .
.It Ev NO_COLOR
Turns colour off under
.Fl -color Ns = Ns Cm auto .
.It Ev XDG_CACHE_HOME
Moves the query cache; see
.Sx FILES .
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa ~/.cache/optique/
Persistent query cache, one JSON Lines file per generation, named
.Pa v<schema>-<tree revision>-<make.conf hash>.jsonl .
The tree revision is the git commit the tree's
.Pa .git
names, or, on a tree without one,
.Ql mtime-
followed by the modification times of
.Pa Mk/bsd.port.mk ,
.Pa MOVED
and
.Pa UPDATING .
Overridden by
.Ev XDG_CACHE_HOME ,
in which case
.Pa $XDG_CACHE_HOME/optique/
is used; an empty or relative
.Ev XDG_CACHE_HOME
is ignored.
Stale generations are pruned automatically.
A change to a file a
.Pa make.conf
includes does not start a new generation; clear the cache with
.Fl -clear-cache
.Pq see Sx CAVEATS .
Disabled entirely by
.Fl -no-cache .
.It Pa ~/.cache/optique/drafts/
Saved staged edits from an interrupted session, one JSON file per options
directory, named after a hash of its path.
.It Pa /tmp/.tmpXXXXXXXX/
Per-run staging directory, honouring
.Ev TMPDIR ,
holding the merged
.Pa make.conf
handed to
.Nm make
and, under
.Pa db/ ,
the staging
.Va PORT_DBDIR
that background re-queries evaluate uncommitted toggles against.
Removed when
.Nm
exits.
.It Pa /usr/local/etc/poudriere.d/
Poudriere configuration root.
.It Pa /usr/local/etc/poudriere.d/ports/<tree>/mnt
File holding the path of the named ports tree, or of an overlay named with
.Fl O .
.It Pa /usr/local/etc/poudriere.d/*options/
Candidate options directories; see
.Sx POUDRIERE INTEGRATION .
.It Pa /usr/local/etc/poudriere.d/*make.conf
The merged
.Pa make.conf
layers.
.It Pa /usr/local/etc/poudriere.d/*blacklist
The merged blacklist chain.
.It Pa /usr/local/etc/synth/synth.ini
.Xr synth 1
configuration, read under
.Fl s
for
.Va Directory_portsdir
and
.Va Directory_options .
.It Pa /usr/local/etc/synth/<profile>-make.conf
The profile's
.Pa make.conf
under
.Fl s .
.It Pa /etc/make.conf
The
.Pa make.conf
of a local build, unless
.Ev __MAKE_CONF
names another.
.It Pa /usr/ports/
The ports tree of a local build when
.Ev PORTSDIR
names none, and of poudriere's implicit
.Ql default
tree when
.Pa poudriere.d
names none.
.It Pa /var/db/ports/
The options directory of a local build when
.Ev PORT_DBDIR
names none.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Cm scan
uses its exit status as a machine-readable gate:
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It 0
Nothing is pending: every port in the closure is configured and consistent, or
is fully decided by
.Pa make.conf ,
or is blacklisted, and no dependency loop is left to untangle.
.It 1
A human decision is pending: at least one port is unconfigured or stale with
undecided options, or has a conflict, or the closure holds a dependency loop
not all of whose members are blacklisted, or a port could not be checked
because its query failed (it, and what it depends on, are missing from the
report).
An options file that exists but cannot be read is such a failure:
.Nm
reports
.Dq cannot read options file Ar path : Ar reason
for its port rather than take it for an unconfigured one, so no command
writes over it, and
.Cm clean
leaves it in place.
.It 3
An error occurred, for example an unreadable package list or an unresolvable
ports tree.
A standard output closed by its reader
.Pq Ql optique scan | head
ends the run the same way, without a message.
It is never 1, so that a failed run cannot pass for a pending decision.
.El
.Pp
.Cm decide
uses its exit status the same way:
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It 0
The plan was honoured: every value asked for is the value recorded, and every
file that had to be written was.
.It 1
The plan was not honoured, and nothing was written: some value was refused or
undone, a port was not in the closure, the result would conflict, or a file
could not be written.
The report on standard output says which.
.It 3
An error occurred, for example a malformed plan or an unresolvable ports tree.
.El
.Pp
.Cm tui ,
.Cm sync
and
.Cm clean
exit 0 on success and 3 on error, with a diagnostic on standard error.
Per-file failures during
.Cm sync
or
.Cm clean
are reported individually and counted in the summary without failing the run.
A file
.Cm sync
cannot write stops it from writing or removing any other: every file is
written aside first and renamed into place only once all of them were.
Invalid command line usage exits 2.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Review the closure of two ports interactively for the poudriere set
.Ql workstation :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
optique -z workstation www/nginx mail/dovecot
.Ed
.Pp
Do the same from poudriere package lists;
.Fl f
is repeatable and combines with origins on the command line:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
optique -z server -f /usr/local/etc/poudriere.d/pkglist
optique -z server -f base-list -f extra-list www/nginx
.Ed
.Pp
Report which ports are unconfigured or stale, as a table or as JSON:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
optique scan -z workstation -f pkglist
optique scan -z workstation --json -f pkglist
.Ed
.Pp
Use the exit status as a
.Xr cron 8
gate that only speaks up when a decision is actually pending:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
optique scan -z ws -q -f list || mail-me
.Ed
.Pp
Refresh every options file without interaction, first reporting, then writing:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
optique sync -z workstation --dry-run -f pkglist
optique sync -z workstation -f pkglist
.Ed
.Pp
Garbage-collect the options directory, progressively more aggressively:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
optique clean -z workstation --dry-run
optique clean -z workstation --redundant
optique clean -z workstation --unused -f pkglist
.Ed
.Pp
Drive it from a script or an agent: read the state with every option explained,
write a plan, check it, then carry it out
.Pq see Sx MACHINE INTERFACE :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
optique scan -z ws --json --options -f list > state.json
echo '{"www/nginx": {"LUA": true}}' > plan.json
optique decide -z ws -n < plan.json
optique decide -z ws    < plan.json
.Ed
.Pp
Turn one option off across a whole package list, without a terminal.
Only the ports that actually carry the option go into the plan: asking for an
option a port does not have is a plan error, not a no-op, so the
.Fl -options
detail is what the filter reads:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
optique scan -z ws --json --options -f list |
    jq '[.ports[]
         | select(any(.options[]; .name == "DOCS"))
         | {(.port): {DOCS: false}}] | add' |
    optique decide -z ws -f list
.Ed
.Pp
A full hygiene pass for one package list, refreshing and then
garbage-collecting, suitable for running before a bulk build:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
optique sync -z ws -f list && \e
    optique clean -z ws --unused --redundant -f list
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr make 1 ,
.Xr synth 1 ,
.Xr ports 7 ,
.Xr poudriere 8
.Sh HISTORY
.Nm
first appeared in 2026 as a replacement for the serial, one-dialog-per-port
workflow of
.Nm poudriere Cm options Fl C .
